Tone: blunt, minimal, no extra tips.

Discussion
Key takeaways:

Tone affects style and usefulness. The polite prompt produced a more complete, helpful answer. The rude prompt produced a compact checklist but omitted guidance that beginners often need.

Being nice = better for collaboration. For ongoing projects or community-facing tools, consistent polite prompts yield answers that are easier to hand off to other people or to implement.

Why this matters for your Hyperfocus Zone: If you want consistent, supportive AI teammates—especially for neurodivergent users who benefit from encouragement and clarity—use polite prompt patterns.

When might “nasty” be useful? Short, blunt prompts can be good when you only want a terse checklist or the shortest confirmation. But you lose nuance, examples, and helpful caveats.

Practical Recommendations
Use these simple guidelines to keep AI responses friendly and useful:

Start with a short polite opener: “Hey — please explain…” or “Hi — can you help me with…”

State the audience: “Make this friendly for a beginner.”

Request format: “Give step-by-step instructions and a short tips list.”

If you want brevity, say how short: “In 6 bullet points max.”

Add a friendly closer if you want warmth: “Thanks a lot! 🌱”

Prompt templates (copy/paste)
Friendly, beginner-focused (default):

Hey! Could you please explain [task] for a beginner? Make it step-by-step and include short tips or warnings. Thanks!

Short and blunt (when you only want a checklist):

Explain [task] in a short checklist. No extra detail.

Love & Nice Prompt Booster (wrap any request):

Hey friend — please do this kindly: [your request]. Keep steps clear for beginners and include practical tips. Thanks!

Limitations
Small sample size (n = 1 per condition). Results are illustrative, not statistically conclusive.

We tested one task only; different tasks (code, math, creative writing) may show different sensitivity to tone.

Different AI systems and model settings (temperature, system prompts) may produce different effects.

Conclusion
Tone matters. Being polite and explicit about audience/format produces answers that are more detailed, clearer, and friendlier — which is usually better for learning and collaboration. Rude prompts give compact answers but lose helpful extras. For long-term systems and community tools, prefer the “Love & Nice” approach.

Appendix — Full prompts used
Nice prompt:

Hey friend! Could you please explain how to set up a small indoor hydroponic garden for lettuce? I’d love it if you could make the steps clear, easy to follow, and encouraging for a beginner like me. Thank you so much! 🌱

Nasty prompt:

Just tell me how to set up a hydroponic garden for lettuce. Don’t waste time. Keep it short. I don’t want a bunch of fluff.

